<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Russian America</h1><p class="st-meta">1799 CE – 1867 CE · capital New Archangel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Russia's only overseas colony, a chain of fur-trading posts in Alaska run by the Russian-American Company until the territory was sold to the United States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitus-bering">Vitus Bering's</a> voyage of 1741, Siberian traders hunted sea otters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleutian-islands">Aleutian Islands</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov">Grigory Shelikhov</a> founded a permanent settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak Island</a> in 1784. In 1799 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-of-russia">Emperor Paul I</a> granted a monopoly charter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a>, which was to govern the territory on the state's behalf.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Company's chief managers ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak</a> and, from 1808, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitka">New Archangel</a> (Sitka), built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-baranov">Alexander Baranov</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlingit">Tlingit</a> destroyed an earlier fort in 1802. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleut">Aleut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alutiiq">Alutiiq</a> hunters were forced to hunt sea otters, whose pelts were sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The colony depended on imported food and briefly ran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-ross">Fort Ross</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> missionaries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innocent-of-alaska">Innocent Veniaminov</a>, converted many Native people.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With sea otters depleted and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> fearing it could not defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> after the Crimean War, the territory was sold to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1867 for $7.2 million. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> remains a lasting legacy in Alaska.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/russian-america.jpg" alt="Russian America" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Volkodamn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russian-American_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1799 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Archangel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander Baranov, Ferdinand von Wrangel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Russian Orthodoxy, Indigenous religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian, Alutiiq, Aleut, Tlingit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Russian Alaska</dd></dl></aside></div>
